    "data": {
      "A": "Research low-risk per-state hot-path optimizations: precompute valid neighbor transitions, avoid scanning all boxes for occupancy, update the sorted box-position key incrementally after a push, and skip full key comparisons when stored hashes differ. Retain full state equality checks and use index types that do not truncate valid board positions. Measure the changes separately; the current source’s box scans, sorting, and hash-table lookup are candidates, not confirmed bottlenecks.",
      "B": "Research search-space reduction using safe deadlock pruning and a push-oriented state representation keyed by box positions plus the player’s reachable region. Preserve shortest total player-movement length—not merely fewest pushes—by charging walking distances and retaining enough path information to produce the required up, down, left, right lexicographic tie-break. Prove pruning cannot discard a valid optimal solution; compare correctness and explored-state counts against the baseline.",
      "C": "Research deterministic parallel expansion of BFS layers on the ARM64 target’s four CPU cores. Preserve shortest-path and tie-break semantics when merging duplicate states and selecting goals; avoid nondeterministic first-writer path selection. The measured baseline is only about 4.75 ms, so test whether parallel overhead is amortized on the available general workload and keep this lane only if end-to-end measurements improve.",
      "G": "Integrate only validated programs from A, B, and C. The e1 baseline is correct on 4 cases, with three timings of 0.0046365, 0.0049767, and 0.0046419 seconds (mean 0.0047517 seconds; reported score 0.5); there is no profiler data or evidence that gains compose. Recheck correctness, shortest length, and the official move-order tie-break, then measure individual candidates and the integrated result under the unchanged harness. Do not assume additive gains or retain a combination that regresses performance or correctness.",
      "analysis": "The source performs breadth-first state search with sorted box positions, dead-square pruning, chained hash lookup, and per-move box scans. The only automatic run evidence is the correct four-case baseline at roughly 4.75 ms on a four-core ARM64 CPU without a GPU; no hot-path profile is available. The role objectives therefore test distinct hypotheses—state-operation cost, search-space size, and parallelism—while preserving general-input correctness and the official shortest-solution/tie-break requirements."
